  "problem": "Expressed in degrees, $\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $1$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1.\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {45}^{∘}={18}^{∘}.\n$$",
